Title:
Windows Copilot API — a free, OpenAI-compatible API powered by Microsoft Copilot

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4–8 sentences max):
- This repository reverse engineers/automates the consumer Copilot web experience (copilot.microsoft.com) and exposes it as a programmable API.  
- It solves the lack of an official free Copilot API by turning a logged-in Copilot session into either (1) a Python client library or (2) a local OpenAI-compatible REST server (`/v1/chat/completions`, `/v1/models`).  
- The project focuses heavily on session automation: browser-based login (Playwright), token/cookie persistence, and handling Cloudflare “cf_clearance” challenges with automatic re-auth/refresh flows.  
- It supports streaming responses and multi-turn conversations via a `conversation_id`, and includes diagnostics plus stress/rate-limit tooling.  
- Useful for red teamers, pentesters, and tool developers who want an OpenAI-compatible local endpoint backed by a personal Copilot account; also relevant to defenders studying web automation, session replay, and Cloudflare gating behavior.  
- Interesting because it operationalizes a consumer web chat into an API surface and documents practical constraints (clearance expiry, serialization, upstream 502s) and mitigations (UA unification, token bucket limiting).

Technical Focus:
- Playwright-driven interactive login and session persistence (cookies/tokens under `session/`)
- Cloudflare Turnstile / `cf_clearance` acquisition, replay, and expiry handling
- OpenAI-compatible API emulation with FastAPI (`/v1/chat/completions`, streaming)
- WebSocket/protocol capture and troubleshooting diagnostics (`ws_capture.log`)
- Concurrency serialization (single-account upstream lock) and stress testing
- Token-bucket rate limiting with `429` + `Retry-After`

Use Cases:
- Run a local “OpenAI API” endpoint backed by a personal Microsoft Copilot account for existing OpenAI-compatible apps/agents
- Programmatic Copilot chat automation from Python with streaming and conversation threading
- Research Cloudflare clearance behavior (UA binding, cookie reuse) and reliability across network types (residential vs DC/VPN)
- Build internal tooling that needs an LLM endpoint without managing API keys (with associated ToS considerations)
- Test client backoff/retry behavior against transient upstream failures (`502`) and local rate limits (`429`)

Keywords:
Microsoft Copilot, copilot.microsoft.com, OpenAI-compatible API, FastAPI, Playwright, Chromium automation, Cloudflare, Turnstile, cf_clearance, cookies, session replay, WebSocket, streaming chat completions, conversation_id, rate limiting, token bucket, Docker, uvicorn, user-agent spoofing, curl_cffi